{"answer":"Temperature control in clean rooms keeps the air temperature uniform and prevents thermal gradients that can drive convection currents. Those currents can stir and transport airborne particles, raising contamination levels and potentially damaging sensitive aerospace components. The Order notes that temperature must be monitored in the 3‑to‑5‑foot zone above the work area and that no readings should be taken within two feet of heat‑producing units to avoid misleading results (P685, 5.7.3).

Humidity control is equally critical. Low humidity (<30 %) can cause static electricity that dislodges or attracts particles, while high humidity (>50 %) can lead to metallic oxidation and other corrosion phenomena that contaminate or degrade equipment. The Order specifies a desired relative humidity range of no less than 30 % and no more than 50 %, and indicates that special control measures should be employed for items that are supersensitive to humidity (P685, 5.7.4). Maintaining this range reduces static build‑up, prevents oxidation, and thereby helps keep particulate and surface contamination within the limits established in Table 3‑1 for class 100,000 clean rooms.

Together, stable temperature and controlled humidity make a clean‑room environment that restrains particle migration, minimizes static and corrosion, and supports the stringent contamination tolerances required in aerospace manufacturing.","citations":["P685"],"insufficient_evidence":false}

{"answer":"Motor‑Driven Handpieces are the rotary handpieces that form part of the 2 M workstation.  They consist of (a) a power‑unit driven handpiece (available only with the PRC‑2000 workstation, shown in Figure 03‑13) and (b) a rechargeable handpiece (available only with the 2 M Portable Kit, shown in Figure 03‑14).  These handpieces provide rotary functions that are powered by either the workstation’s power unit or by a rechargeable battery.

Microminiature Specific Handpieces are a separate set of handpieces that are also part of the 2 M workstation but are designed specifically for microminiature work and are only available with the PRC‑2000 platform.  They include a thermal tweezers (shown in Figure 03‑15) and a vacuum pick device (shown in Figure 03‑16).  These tools are used for handling very small components and applying precise heat or picking force.

In short: Motor‑driven handpieces provide rotary functions for general 2 M work and are available on both the PRC‑2000 and the 2 M Portable Kit, while microminiature specific handpieces provide thermal tweezers and a vacuum pick device for microminiature work and are only available on the PRC‑2000 workstation.","citations":["P2131"],"insufficient_evidence":false}

{"answer":"Emergency shutdown procedures are performed immediately when an abnormal or hazardous condition is detected. As per the AFTF manual, the sequence is: activate the emergency‑shutdown switch (or handheld Safe‑T‑Stop) in the OFF position, close all burner control ball valves, position all ignitor switches to OFF, verify that all personnel have evacuated the burn area, purge trapped LPG from the lines, lock‑tag inoperable components, and notify the appropriate agency for corrective action. 

Post‑shutdown (system shutdown and cleanup) is carried out after the training exercise is finished. It includes the emergency steps but adds additional cleanup actions: close the manifold shut‑off valve, burn off all residual LPG by igniting predetermined burners and opening the corresponding burner control valves, close all LPG burner control valves after the fire burns out, position ignitor switches to OFF, determine that the area is safe, and finally push the emergency shutdown switch (or handheld Safe‑T‑Stop) into the OFF position. 

The key difference is that emergency shutdown is a rapid safety‑first action to stop the system and ensure personnel safety, whereas post‑shutdown includes the same safety measures plus additional steps for defueling, burn‑off, and confirming the training area is safe before leaving the facility.","citations":["P191","P214","P222"],"insufficient_evidence":false}
